#### cov / corrcoef: rowvar, bias, ddof, y, complex conjugation, clipping, constant / NaN rows
ok   cov(X.T, rowvar=False) == exact covariance of the columns (ddof=1)
ok   cov(bias=True) normalises by N
ok   cov(ddof=2) normalises by N - 2
ok   cov(bias=True, ddof=1): 'ddof ... overrides the value implied by bias' -> N - 1
ok   cov(x, y) = cov of the stacked variables (5 x 5)
ok   cov(x.T, y.T, rowvar=False): y's columns appended as variables
ok   cov(1-D x, 1-D y) -> 2 x 2
ok   cov(1-D x) -> 0-d variance with ddof 1
ok   cov(complex) = sum (x_i - m_i) conj(x_j - m_j) / (N - 1), exact
ok   cov(complex) is Hermitian with a real positive diagonal up to rounding (1e-14 x max|C|)
   cov(complex) measured: max |C - C^H| = 7.1e-15, max |Im C_ii| = 1.7e-16 (max |C| = 69.1); not exactly Hermitian (matrix product rounding)
ok   corrcoef(complex) = C_ij / sqrt(C_ii C_jj)
ok   corrcoef(complex): real and imaginary parts within [-1, 1] (clipped)
ok   corrcoef of 300 exactly (anti-)correlated pairs with large offsets: every |r| <= 1 (clipped)  max |r| 1.0
ok   corrcoef with a constant row: that row and column NaN, RuntimeWarning, the rest finite  ['RuntimeWarning']
ok   corrcoef with a NaN in row 1: row / column 1 NaN, the others exact
ok   corrcoef(bias=...) is accepted but deprecated (DeprecationWarning) and has no effect  ['DeprecationWarning']
ok   cov(dtype=float32) returns float32
ok   corrcoef(dtype=float32) returns float32
ok   cov(fweights non-integer) raises TypeError  TypeError: fweights must be integer
ok   cov(fweights negative) raises ValueError  ValueError: fweights cannot be negative
ok   cov(aweights negative) raises ValueError  ValueError: aweights cannot be negative
ok   cov with a single observation (N - ddof = 0): RuntimeWarning 'Degrees of freedom <= 0' and non-finite result  [[nan, nan], [nan, nan]] ['Degrees of freedom <= 0 for slice']
#### float16 mean, complex var / std, median of ints, percentile q array + out, quantile q range, count_nonzero, apply_over_axes
ok   mean(float16): 'float16 results are computed using float32 intermediates' -> exact float16(0.1) for 10000 copies  0.1
ok   mean(float16) of 20000 values in [0, 100) equals float16(exact mean) (within 1 float16 ulp)  50.03125 vs 50.03158794313669
   sum(float16, 20000 values) = inf (exact 1000631.7588627338 exceeds float16 max 65504); mean(dtype=float16) = inf (explicit float16 accumulation overflows; no float32 promise when dtype is given)
ok   var(complex) = mean(|z - mean|^2), a real float64 (documented 'absolute value is taken before squaring')  39.1358024691358
ok   std(complex, ddof=1) = sqrt(sum |z - mean|^2 / (n - 1))
ok   var(complex64) returns float32
ok   median of an even-length int array is the float mean of the middle two: [1, 2, 3, 4] -> 2.5 float64
ok   median(int8 [100, 120]) = 110.0 (no int8 overflow in the averaging)
ok   median(uint8 [250, 254, 1, 3]) = (3 + 250) / 2 = 126.5
ok   percentile(q 2-D (2, 2), axis=1): shape q.shape + reduced shape = (2, 2, 3), exact  (2, 2, 3)
ok   percentile(out=o) returns o, filled with the same values
ok   percentile with an out of the wrong shape raises  ValueError: operands could not be broadcast together with shapes (2,2,3) (2,2,3) (3,2) 
ok   quantile(q=-0.01) outside [0, 1] raises ValueError ('Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]')  ValueError: Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]
ok   quantile(q=1.01) outside [0, 1] raises ValueError ('Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]')  ValueError: Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]
ok   quantile(q=nan) outside [0, 1] raises ValueError ('Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]')  ValueError: Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]
ok   percentile(q=100.5) raises ValueError  ValueError: Percentiles must be in the range [0, 100]
ok   quantile(q=[0.5, 2.0]) raises ValueError (one entry out of range)  ValueError: Quantiles must be in the range [0, 1]
ok   count_nonzero(axis=(0, 2), keepdims=True): shape (1, 2, 1); NaN counts as nonzero, -0.0 as zero  [2, 3]
ok   count_nonzero(axis=1, keepdims=True): shape (3, 1, 2)
ok   count_nonzero(axis=None) -> int total
ok   apply_over_axes(np.sum, a, [0, 2]) == a.sum(axis=(0, 2), keepdims=True) (dims re-inserted, ndim kept)
ok   apply_over_axes with a shape-preserving func (cumsum): repeated application
ok   apply_over_axes(np.mean, a, 1) with a scalar axis
ok   apply_over_axes with a func dropping all dims raises ValueError  ValueError: function is not returning an array of the correct shape
